          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"hamsun-pan-bc3c8d656c175af9\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"Eva’s answer straightforwardly avows her love as the reason for Mack’s punitive treatment. It presents a loving state as true, but does not itself undertake a commitment. Although her voice is later described as trembling and songlike, the words answer a question deliberately and refer to an earlier disclosure, so there is no independent evidence of expressive/reflex-like core force.\",\"ambiguity\":null},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Eva directly tells the narrator, in a causal answer, “Because I love you.”\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Eva explains that Mack treats her and her husband harshly because she loves the narrator and has told Mack so. Her prior disclosure and willingness to endure the consequences reinforce the seriousness of the avowal, while the downward glance and trembling, songlike voice convey emotional vulnerability. Those delivery features do not change the core utterance from a truth-conditional avowal into a reflexive discharge or relational undertaking.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Fordi jeg elsker dig.” (“Because I love you.”)\",\"supports\":\"The utterance explicitly presents Eva’s love for her addressee as the true cause of Mack’s conduct, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":1.0},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The utterance directly answers the repeated question “Hvorfor gjør han det?” (“Why does he do that?”).\",\"supports\":\"Its explanatory role supports a deliberate truth-conditional avowal rather than a reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Eva immediately adds, “Jeg har sagt ham det.” (“I have told him.”)\",\"supports\":\"Her reference to an earlier disclosure suggests an established, communicable claim about her feelings, further supporting T and weighing against E.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"After the exchange, her voice is described as sounding “ganske som en liten skjælvende sang” (“exactly like a little trembling song”).\",\"supports\":\"This establishes emotionally vulnerable delivery but, without evidence that the words escaped reflexively, does not independently support E.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as a present-tense, affirmative explicit love construction in Knut Hamsun’s Pan.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the identified core event and its ordinary present-state linguistic form.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the explanatory avowal. Emotional delivery and consequential context are adequately recorded contextually; they create no residual pressure requiring E, P, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
